# Desktop UI endgame: Views + Skia

**This rule is always on (`alwaysApply: true`).** The chosen high-ceiling UI is in-process C++ **Chromium-style Views** (widget / layout / events) plus **Skia** (paint), hosting the existing map viewport (`src/render`, `src/map` / `src/feature`). Details: `docs/superpowers/ui-views-skia.md`.

## Do

- New shell toolkit lives in `src/ui/views/` (`ui::views`). Skia backend stub in `src/ui/gfx/` (`ui::gfx`).
- Product hosts live under `src/app/` (`app/views`, `app/winui`). Do not reintroduce `src/chrome/` or a WebView2 / `src/web` stack.
- Keep the map as a hosted viewport (HWND / native view) on the existing C++ render + GIS stack.
- Treat `src/ui/{gui,mfc_ex,xview,xcatalog,xambox}` and leftover MFC sources in `src/app/` as **legacy**. Feature Pack (`CMFC*`) is a bridge to get an exe, not the destination.
- Treat Skia as a **canvas / render backend** only, never as a widget kit.

## Don’t

- **Don’t** introduce Qt (see `no-qt.mdc`).
- **Don’t** treat WinUI 3, WinAppSDK, or WebView2 as the default or endgame shell.
- **Don’t** treat MFC Feature Pack as the endgame UI toolkit.
- **Don’t** pull leftover MFC / `src/legacy/` into Views product TUs (see **`legacy-unidirectional.mdc`**).
- **Don’t** vendor Chromium or Skia wholesale in drive-by edits.
